2
00:00:11,072 --> 00:00:17,482
Ask an AI to fix one typo in your résumé,
and it can quietly change a word you chose
on purpose.

3
00:00:17,532 --> 00:00:23,282
Not because the new word is worse — often
it's fine — but because nothing in the
system was stopping it.

4
00:00:23,332 --> 00:00:27,502
Hold on to one line: led a team of six.
You picked led. You meant led.

5
00:00:27,552 --> 00:00:35,972
The obvious explanation is that the model
judged your word and preferred its own — a
taste problem, fixable by telling it to

6
00:00:36,022 --> 00:00:43,052
But it does this even when told not to,
and — the part that gives it away — it
does this when its version is more correct

7
00:00:43,102 --> 00:00:48,922
If taste were the problem, a truer word
would be a better outcome; when the text
is fixed, it's still wrong.

8
00:00:48,972 --> 00:00:52,252
Here's why: these models have no copy
button.

9
00:00:52,302 --> 00:00:58,632
When you ask for the whole paragraph back,
every untouched word is re-predicted, one
at a time, from scratch.

10
